pfep_description_raw:
The biosphere relevant to Kristallin-I is that of the near-surface environment of the upper Rhine valley.  Current understanding of the regional hydrogeology of Northern Switzerland indicates that, for a repository located anywhere in the areas under consideration in the crystalline basement, radionuclide release is most likely to occur somewhere in this region. The most relevant, present-day features of this biosphere are:
 •  topography: the topography of the Rhine valley is characterised by relatively gently sloping valley bottoms, with river terraces developed over gravels laid down in the Quaternary period;
 •  hydrology: the water flow in the Rhine is 3 x 1010 m3 y-1; that in the gravels is 6 x 106 m3 y-1;
 •  climate: the climate is temperate; the mean annual rainfall is 790 mm y-1.

In assessment modelling, these present-day conditions are assumed to persist over the entire period under consideration. However, data relating to the present-day distribution of human settlements, economy, land use and agriculture are not directly used in the biosphere model. Rather, a conservative assumption of subsistence agriculture (with a correspondingly lower population density) is made.

